## Report
Crash/asan attempt: tried cross-building the checksum crate for aarch64 with pure-rust to run a crashing PoC, but the target toolchain is missing (`cargo build -p checksum --no-default-features --features pure-rust --target aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu` failed with `can't find crate for 'std'` and suggested `rustup target add aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`). Valgrind/gdb attempts failed because the tools are not installed (`valgrind: command not found`, `gdb: command not found`). Host x86_64 tests (`cargo test -p checksum --no-default-features --features pure-rust`) pass but do not exercise the aarch64 path.

Code review shows the suspected issue: `crc64::extend` enables the aarch64 PMULL path solely on `is_aarch64_feature_detected!("aes")` and immediately calls `extend_pmull_aarch64` with no PMULL feature check (crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:78-93). The PMULL implementation uses `vmull_p64`/`vmull_high_p64` (PMULL instructions) in the aarch64 module (crc64.rs:365-371, 398-409), yet the entrypoints are annotated only with `#[target_feature(enable = "neon,aes")]` (crc64.rs:415-428). This means CPUs that advertise AES but not PMULL will still execute PMULL instructions once AES is detected, causing an illegal-instruction crash. The logic matches the reported vulnerability even though I could not execute it in this x86_64 container.

