# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
The bug is real (AES-only gate for PMULL instructions), but it is limited to the non-default pure-rust build that the README states is test-only, and it only impacts availability. These constraints reduce likelihood and overall impact compared to the original high rating.
## Likelihood
low - Requires a non-default pure-rust build plus aarch64 hardware that supports AES but not PMULL, making real-world reachability less common even though triggering is trivial once conditions are met.
## Impact
medium - Successful triggering causes an illegal instruction crash of the single-node server, resulting in loss of availability until restart; no data confidentiality or integrity impact is shown.
## Assumptions
- The deployment is built with the non-default `pure-rust` feature (or otherwise enables `checksum/pure-rust`).
- The server runs on aarch64 hardware that reports AES but not PMULL support.
- An attacker can access the S3 HTTP endpoint with valid credentials or via a public bucket to trigger normal object read/write/copy paths that compute CRC64.
- An illegal instruction on the target platform terminates the process (no signal handler that masks SIGILL).
- pure-rust checksum feature enabled
- aarch64 CPU with AES but no PMULL
- CRC64 computation during request handling
## Path
Remote client -> HTTP listener -> CRC64 update -> PMULL fast path (AES-only gate) -> SIGILL crash
## Path evidence
- `crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:80-96` - Runtime dispatch enables the aarch64 PMULL path based only on AES feature detection.
- `crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:365-428` - PMULL intrinsics are used while the functions are only annotated with `neon,aes` target features.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:1600-1609` - CRC64 is updated during streaming reads, showing checksum computation is on request-driven paths.
- `README.md:33-56` - The `pure-rust` feature is documented as a non-default, test-only build variant.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:24-62` - Default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000, indicating local-only exposure by default.
## Narrative
In the pure-rust checksum backend, the aarch64 fast path checks only for the AES feature before calling the PMULL implementation. The PMULL code uses `vmull_p64`/`vmull_high_p64` instructions but is annotated with `#[target_feature(enable = "neon,aes")]`, so on CPUs that expose AES without PMULL, a normal CRC64 computation (triggered by object read/write/copy requests) will execute unsupported PMULL instructions and crash the process. This is reachable from normal S3 operations but only when the non-default `pure-rust` build is used, which the README states is a test-only configuration.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication and authorization for S3 requests
- Configurable max connections and in-flight request limits
- Optional TLS termination
## Blindspots
- No runtime verification on aarch64 hardware; impact inferred from instruction usage.
- No deployment manifests to confirm whether pure-rust builds are used in production or whether the service is publicly exposed.
